{"candidates":[{"content":"The Pragmatic Wager (a0b919ae506615d2) depends on the rejection of inductive probability as a necessary condition for rational action. This relation is refuted if it can be demonstrated that preferring the better-corroborated theory logically entails an assumption that the future will resemble the past, thereby making the 'pragmatic wager' indistinguishable from the inductive principle Popper explicitly rejects.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"5760eda6c518c3d1","quote":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without induction because it is a pragmatic wager, not an epistemic probability claim."},{"block":"07ec28399009e60b","quote":"The analogy of betting on a horse that has won every race fails under Popperian scrutiny because, unlike a horse race, scientific testing does not provide a statistical basis for predicting future outcomes."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"9993f9796f5b92ef528fa5cbfdd9c57c92467ca2bcdb42ecd19cff284df50588"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e1478cc8cfc00c1bff41b052607ab154d47e6602a1a537a5732816bde4eb0dac"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"Verisimilitude as a Regulative Ideal (92968bc0cf42d965) reduces to the methodological commitment that science aims at truth rather than certainty. This relation is refuted if it can be shown that the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) cannot be formally defined without implicitly relying on inductive probability, thereby making the shift from probability to verisimilitude merely a semantic disguise for the very induction Popper rejected.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"4367b2ed8c73936e","quote":"The defense lies in shifting the goal from 'probability' to 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness)."},{"block":"26d5b990b5bc8574"}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"9993f9796f5b92ef528fa5cbfdd9c57c92467ca2bcdb42ecd19cff284df50588"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e1478cc8cfc00c1bff41b052607ab154d47e6602a1a537a5732816bde4eb0dac"}],"typicality":0.5}],"simulation